# sbx-kit on Docker sbx

sbx-kit is a **host-side convenience layer** on the Docker **sbx** CLI. It does not replace sbx. sbx runs sandboxes; sbx-kit manages catalog directories, recipe manifests, bindings, and portable state.

Requires sbx >= {{ .MinSbx }} (kit verify >= {{ .MinKitVerify }}). Check with `sbx-kit version`.

## Layers

| Layer | Commands |
|-------|----------|
| Catalog | `setup`, `catalog add\|ls\|status\|update` |
| Recipes | `recipes`, `recipes create`, `recipes skill`, `recipes verify`, `recipes image …` |
| Box | `box run`, `box bindings`, `box check`, `box upgrade`, `box rm`, `box state` |
| Project | `project readme` |

## Glossary

- **catalog** — path from `setup` (default `~/sbx-kit-catalog`; env `SBX_KIT_CATALOG`)
- **directory** — child of catalog with `recipes/`, optional `kits/`, `images/`
- **recipe** — `<dir>/<name>` from `recipes/agents.yaml`
- **kit** — create-time YAML under `kits/` — **schema and verify belong to sbx**
- **image** — Dockerfile or registry tag under `images/`
- **box** — one sbx sandbox bound to a host project path

## Typical workflow

```bash
sbx-kit setup
sbx-kit catalog add <git-url>    # or: recipes create <dir>
sbx-kit recipes
sbx-kit recipes verify
sbx-kit box run <dir>/<name> --yes
sbx-kit box bindings
```

## Verify (who owns what)

```bash
sbx-kit recipes verify [id]           # manifest + optional sbx kit verify
sbx-kit recipes verify --skip-kits    # manifest only
sbx-kit recipes verify kits [dir]     # sbx kit verify only
```

- Recipe manifests → **sbx-kit**
- Kit specs → **sbx** (`sbx kit verify`, migrate with sbx)
- Runtime secrets → **sbx** (`sbx secret set`); hints via `sbx-kit box check`

